{
 "id": "2026-liu-night",
 "name": "Night Lighting in Gardens and Parks: Restoration Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 20 night photographs from Figure 3 of Liu, Gong & Dai (2026), \"Nighttime lighting effects on perceived restoration depend on landscape type: evidence from classical garden landscapes and modern park landscapes\", Frontiers in Psychology 17: 1958901 (CC BY 4.0). Five scenes from classical gardens in Suzhou and five from modern parks in Xuzhou, each photographed on a full-moon night with the park lights off, plus an artificial-lighting version edited by the authors in Photoshop.\n\n2 × 2 between-participants design: each participant is randomly assigned to one condition (modern park or classical garden × natural night light or artificial lighting) and sees that condition's five photographs in random order. After 2 minutes of quiet sitting, each photo is shown for 30 seconds (\"imagine yourself in the scene\"), followed by the eight-item Short-version Revised Restoration Scale (SRRS; emotional, physiological, cognitive and behavioural restoration; 1 = totally disagree, 9 = totally agree) and a one-minute rest. Records are keyed by the paper's image id (park_natural_01 … garden_artificial_20); the page source holds the paper's condition means and model estimates. Analysis as in the paper: reverse-code q3 and q4, average the four dimensions into an overall score, and fit a linear mixed model with landscape type × lighting and participant and base-scene random intercepts.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online instead of individually in a lab; (2) images are taken from the published figure (about 670 × 373 px), which is lower resolution than the originals; (3) assignment to conditions is random per participant rather than a fixed allocation sequence of 50 per condition; (4) English wording of the SRRS items as given in the paper's Table 1 (the study used Chinese); (5) the 15-expert landscape-feature ratings are not part of the template.",
